  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes and evaluates a synergetic route for Pb₀.₉₂Ba₀.₀₈[Zr₀.₅₀₊ₓTi₀.₄₈₋ₓ(Nb₀.₅Sb₀.₅)₀.₀₂]O₃ ceramics: adjust the Zr/Ti ratio to engineer the morphotropic phase boundary and combine tape casting, lamination, and hot pressing as a densification-focused process sequence to reduce pores and oxygen vacancies, with the aim of concurrently enhancing piezoelectric performance and temperature stability.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Texturing provides a mechanistic clue for improving piezoelectricity and temperature stability because the reported high texturing degree and large grain size enable more sufficient domain switching, saturated poling, and stable phase/domain configurations."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The combined evidence motivates a research strategy that integrates MPB phase-boundary engineering with densification-oriented process engineering to address the piezoelectricity versus temperature-stability trade-off."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The application demand for piezoceramics motivates developing materials that combine excellent piezoelectric properties with good temperature stability for reliable piezoelectric devices."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) A central unresolved challenge is the trade-off between high piezoelectricity and temperature stability, since piezoelectric enhancement is commonly associated with structural instability whereas temperature stability favors a stable structure."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) In random PZT-based ceramics, generated pores and oxygen vacancies are defect sources that can hinder saturated poling and domain switching, thereby worsening piezoelectric and temperature-stability behavior."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Effectively eliminating pores and reducing oxygen vacancies in PZT-based ceramics is a plausible strategy for obtaining high piezoelectricity together with improved temperature stability."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The study combines phase-boundary engineering with the three process techniques to pursue simultaneous improvement of piezoelectricity and temperature stability through a synergetic design."];
  "conc_phase" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is Zr/Ti adjusted to establish MPB [s18], and if MPB flattens Landau energy with multi-phase coexistence [s19], then enhanced dielectric response boosting piezoelectricity."];
  "conc_process" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is tape casting/lamination/hot pressing applied to MPB composition [s20], and if process reduces pores/oxygen vacancies [s26], then improved temperature stability (and further piezoelectric improvement)."];
  "impl_combined_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If phase-boundary engineering and process engineering are synergistically combined on the optimal MPB composition (distinct from single-technique literatures with limited enhancement), then simultaneous boost of piezoelectricity and temperature stability is achieved."];
  "impl_defect_knowledge" [label="(0,0,0) Knowledge: pores reduce density/E_b and oxygen vacancies form dipole defects (V_Pb''-V_O••) that pin room-temperature domain switching and de-pin at high temperature due to enhanced migration."];
  "impl_demand_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If piezoceramics are important functional materials widely used in vital applications and technology rapidly develops demanding better performance, then piezoceramics must possess both excellent piezoelectric properties and good temperature stability to meet device requirements."];
  "impl_eliminate_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If pores and oxygen vacancies are effectively eliminated/reduced while maintaining economical practicality superior to texturing, then both high piezoelectricity and improved temperature stability become highly anticipated."];
  "impl_thermo_knowledge" [label="(0,0,0) Knowledge: thermodynamic theory shows that phase-boundary engineering at MPB flattens Landau energy profile with nanoscale multi-phase coexistence, while process engineering (tape casting + lamination + hot pressing) achieves denser microstructure and reduced PbO volatilization/oxygen vacancies."];
  "impl_tradeoff_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If high piezoelectricity is obtained by introducing structural instability while high temperature stability prefers a stable structure, then the trade-off stems from their different contributions, previous instability strategies inevitably decrease T_C, and random ceramics suffer from pores/vacancies hindering poling."];
  "s10" [label="(10,0,0) The high texturing degree and the large grain size make the domain switching more sufficiently and the degree of poling more saturated, resulting in the stable phase structure and domain configurations."];
  "s12" [label="(12,0,0) In addition to the above, a large number of pores and oxygen vacancies will be generated in random PZT-based ceramics due to the high sintering temperature and the volatilization of Pb element."];
  "s15" [label="(15,0,0) Conversely, if one can effectively eliminate the pores and reduce the content of oxygen vacancies in PZT-based ceramics, both high piezoelectricity and improved temperature stability are highly anticipated."];
  "s18" [label="(18,0,0) For the Strategy 1, we elaborately adjusted the Zr/Ti ratio to establish the morphotropic phase boundary (MPB) in PBZTNS-x ceramics."];
  "s19" [label="(19,0,0) The flattened Landau energy profile at the MPB and the nanoscale multi-phase coexistence endow PBZTNS-x ceramics with enhanced dielectric response, consequently boosting the piezoelectricity."];
  "s20" [label="(20,0,0) For the Strategy 2, based on the optimal composition selected by the Strategy 1, we employed the tape casting, lamination, and hot pressing techniques to achieve a denser microstructure and reduce the PbO volatilization and the content of oxygen vacancies, thereby improving the temperature stability."];
  "s26" [label="(26,0,0) The process engineering significantly reduces the content of pores and oxygen vacancies, thereby further improving piezoelectric properties but boosting the temperature stability."];
  "s3" [label="(3,0,0) In particular, piezoelectric devices require piezoceramics possessing excellent piezoelectric properties and good temperature stability to ensure sensitivity and reliable outputs."];
  "s35" [label="(35,0,0) Here, based on the Strategy 1, we combined three process techniques together to achieve this goal by significantly improving the density and reducing the PbO volatilization, which was rarely reported."];
  "s7" [label="(7,0,0) Generally, the high piezoelectricity is usually obtained by introducing structural instability, while the high temperature stability prefers a stable structure."];
  "s9" [label="(9,0,0) Intriguingly, even though the complex preparation process and high cost, the texturing method did significantly enhance both piezoelectricity and temperature stability even without increasing the T_C."];
